    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d774">

                <pb facs="0827" n="729" sameAs="frus:frus1932v04/pg729"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">123 C 353/203: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Mukden (Myers) to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Mukden</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-01-05T11:00:00+08:00">January 5, 1932—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received January 5—2:52 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Department’s telegram of January 4, 2 p.m.<anchor n="42" xml:id="d774-f1"/>
                    Supplementing my full report of developments to date telegraphed to the Legation
                    last <pb facs="0828" n="730" xml:id="pg730"/> evening, Acting Japanese Consul
                    General yesterday assured me that adequate measures had been taken to prevent
                    the repetition of such an incident.</p>
                <p>Japanese apparently attempting to place onus of blame for the attack on the
                    dismissed civilian army interpreter although at least one soldier
                    participated.</p>
                <p>In recent months there have been a number of cases wherein American citizens were
                    subjected to grave incivility by truculent Japanese police or soldiers. One case
                    of this kind was taken up with Japanese Consul General on October 15th for which
                    suitable amends were made.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Myers</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
